## 5. Runtime Configuration and Normal Operation

Normal configuration required to install, deploy, integrate, administer, and operate the Software is permitted and is not considered a prohibited Modification.

You may change configuration values exposed by the Software for their intended operational purpose.

Permitted configuration changes may include:

* IP addresses;
* hostnames;
* network interfaces;
* ports;
* service addresses;
* storage locations;
* database locations;
* filesystem paths;
* mount points;
* log destinations;
* syslog settings;
* service names;
* user names;
* group names;
* file ownership;
* permitted filesystem permissions;
* certificate profiles;
* certificate validity periods;
* certificate subject parameters;
* certificate SAN parameters;
* certificate templates;
* certificate policies;
* key algorithms;
* key sizes;
* signature algorithms;
* approved cryptographic configuration;
* Root CA configuration;
* Intermediate CA configuration;
* certificate-chain configuration;
* CRL settings;
* CRL distribution points;
* OCSP settings;
* OCSP responder addresses;
* certificate enrollment settings;
* certificate renewal settings;
* certificate revocation settings;
* HSM configuration;
* PKCS#11 configuration;
* TPM configuration;
* SoftHSM configuration;
* cryptographic provider configuration;
* authentication settings;
* authorization settings;
* access-control settings;
* API settings;
* service parameters;
* startup parameters;
* environment variables;
* retention periods;
* documented policy values; and
* other parameters expressly intended to be configured by an administrator.

You may also perform normal administrative operations, including:

* starting services;
* stopping services;
* restarting services;
* enabling or disabling startup;
* creating a Root CA;
* creating an Intermediate CA;
* issuing certificates;
* renewing certificates;
* revoking certificates;
* publishing CRLs;
* responding to OCSP requests;
* exporting public certificates;
* exporting certificate chains;
* exporting certificate bundles;
* generating cryptographic keys through documented mechanisms;
* configuring HSMs or cryptographic modules;
* backing up authorized data;
* restoring authorized backups;
* rotating certificates or cryptographic keys through documented procedures;
* configuring service accounts;
* applying official updates supplied by the Licensor; and
* operating documented maintenance procedures.

These operations are permitted only when performed using the functionality and configuration mechanisms provided by the unmodified Software.

This Section does not authorize changes to:

* source code;
* executable code;
* object code;
* internal program logic;
* certificate-validation logic;
* security-enforcement logic;
* key-protection logic; or
* scripts containing implementation logic.

